ecause  they're not white europeans the british  judicial system the british don't care  about the fact that these are murdered  black africans they only care about  white europeans that's why they take  such a strong line in ukraine and as a  as a british member of pond i'm very  offended by that but the evidence is  there on the table 16 years they've not  been sent back to rwanda as so many  other countries have done with those who  the rwandan government have sought to  extradite and they're not being tried  under the british system either and the  police say it will take years and years  to address this point and it isn't good  enough and it's very bad for britain's  international reputation and what did  you make of the prime minister's  response to your question  well i mean you know he he he he's not  obviously very familiar with this issue  but he promised that he would get  familiar with this issue and that was my  aim and purpose that when he goes down  to rwanda undoubtedly you know the  president of ryan will say to him you're  very happy to send your asylum seekers  here why aren't you uh either  prosecuting in the uk or sending back to  rwanda these people who are alleged to  have committed the most terrible crimes  against our citizens you mentioned there  the deal uh struck between the uk and  rwanda to send asylum seekers to rwanda  the first plane due to leave next week  but of course it's had this challenge  in the high courts and do you think that  policy needs to be dropped  well i i think that the rawannas have a  very good reputation for helping out  with humanitarian situations they have  taken peop desperate people who were  caught off the coast of libya from the  struggle there and give them a new life  in in rwanda and the rwandans are trying  to be helpful in a humanitarian way they  did the same thing for the israelis but  my issue with the policy is that i don't  think it will work and it's incredibly  expensive for the british taxpayer i'm  not opposed to  britain if someone comes here illegally  seeks asylum is processed in the uk and  is then turned down for asylum if if  then the british wish to deport them to  rwanda and rwanda is willing to take  them i wouldn't i wouldn't be against  that but this is a policy which will be  extremely difficult to implement will be  hideously expensive for taxpayers and i  just don't think it will work that's my  main objection  how popular do you think these kind of  policies are with the electorate it felt  like for years in a way and being tough  on immigration was being seen as a bit  of a badge of honour something that  would always go down well with with the  population uh at large do you think  that's changed well i think you know i  think that this is uh  i think this has brought up a lot of  people up short it's just not the sort  of thing we would normally do and and  actually you know there's no alternative  but to do the hard yards of processing  people properly employing more staff to  process them when they get here having a  better relationship with the french i  mean you can't do anything about the  core problem which is people coming  across the channel in a leaky boat  putting themselves into the hands of the  modern day equivalent of the slave  trader you've got you've got that's the  prime problem you've got to have a deal  with the french so so that's the second  thing you need to do and you need to  revise the  1951 geneva convention on refugees  because that was set up before there was  mass travel before there was climate  change driven migration um and and  there's no there's really no alternative  uh but to do those hard difficult things  if you're going to get this problem  under control and say you're going to  ship a few of them off halfway around  the world you know it's it's not very  nice and it's not really the way britain  does these things another policy where  you need to do the hard yards is what's  happening in northern ireland and the  impact of the northern ireland protocol  there and lots of people extremely  concerned about it the whole  administration of course not up and  running until it is sorted now the  government is claiming that it's legal  for it to act unilaterally to change  parts of the protocol um do you think in  your view it is well i'm not a lawyer so  i i don't know i would rely on  independent uh legal advice on that  point but uh i do think that the  european union is being extremely  difficult i think they need to be much  more sympathetic and understanding about  the issue of the border in northern  ireland and the problem this is creating  which which we do not want to imperil  the peace agreements that have been set  in in northern ireland uh and i've been  in the house comments long enough to  remember those terrible statements made  after atrocities took place you know we  need to honor the agreements that have  been reached so i think the eu need to  be more sensitive but equally you know  you don't sign a big international  treaty and then a few months later  decide you want to go back on your word  and the last thing britain needs now  when our economy is so stretched at the  moment and there are these huge  difficulties with the cost of living is  to have a trade war with europe where  the americans our closest ally are on  the side of europe so this is a time now  for very cool heads very good  negotiation and a real understanding by  both sides of the issues here but but i  do think the european union is not being  as helpful or as sensitive to these big  international problems as we have a  right to expect  now of course uh this week i think 41 of  conservative mps voted to say they had  no confidence in boris johnson you were  one of them  what would you like to see from boris  johnson going forward to  get your trust back on him  well i think he needs to govern in the  same way he did when he was mayor of  london he needs to  uh  up his game as prime minister and the  problems he's seeking to address i think  i think there's a whole range of things  he needs to do there's a lack of  coherence and a lot of policy these are  very big issues to do with energy costs  the cost of living inflation growth you  know the critical thing in the british  economy is to increase the level of  growth and we need a laser-like  attention from the prime minister and  you know if 32 colleagues change their  minds he will be out of office but uh we  need to get on with tackling the  problems that my constituents have sent  me to westminster to try and address and  not go on about this day after day do  you think there could be another vote  then you said that it just tick nick's  another 32 to change their minds well  there won't be another vote at the  moment for a year and we've got to  respect the conventions that says things  stand but there's there's a lot of  events you know in politics it's events  which come around the corner and  surprise you  and the key thing is he's got to get on  with the job now of delivering the best  possible  answers to these very very serious  problems and this sort of lack of grip  on policy has got to be addressed okay  thank you very much for being on the  program today thank you andrew mitchell  there  you're watching the take we are live in  westminster next we're going to round up  everything we just heard and do a bit of  post-match analysis with our deputy  political editor  [Music]  hello welcome back live to westminster  after a pretty tumultuous week you are  watching the take  we've had lots of takes uh this evening  uh time now for our regular post match  uh roundup with our deputy political  editor at sam coats who's with us here  sam what i'm really intrigued by is you  know we've we've heard from you know our  panel we've heard from concert for mps  where is number 10 at  well i think that number 10 have a  massive decision to make now you heard  on your show all of your in